<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/foundational/value-colour-composition">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every image — cave painting or cinema frame — is built from the same three levers:
    <strong>value</strong> (how light or dark), <strong>colour</strong> (which hue, how intense)
    and <strong>composition</strong> (where the viewer's eye is sent). Beginners chase colour;
    professionals chase value. Squint at any masterwork and it becomes a grey-scale pattern — if
    that pattern reads clearly, the image works at any size.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="colour as a conversation, 350 years long — physics, perception and paint trading ideas" widget="timeline" config={colourTimeline} />

  <h2>The three levers, in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Value does the structure.</strong> A strong image usually has a clear value plan — a light shape against dark, or the reverse. Notan, the Japanese light–dark design study, is the classic drill.</li>
    <li><strong>Colour does the temperature.</strong> Warm advances, cool recedes; complementary pairs (orange–blue, red–green) create vibration when placed side by side — Chevreul's contrast in action.</li>
    <li><strong>Composition does the directing.</strong> Contrast, edges and leading lines steer the eye in a deliberate order — the artist composes the <em>sequence of looking</em>, not just the picture.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Rules, and their honest limits</h2>
  <p>
    The "rule of thirds", the golden ratio, the colour wheel: these are training wheels, not
    physics. Great artists break all of them — Caravaggio drowned his compositions in darkness,
    Hokusai flattened space entirely. The rule exists to be <em>broken knowingly</em>: first make
    the image that follows the principle, then decide what breaking it buys you.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Newton, Opticks (1704); Chevreul (1839); Itten, The Art of Color (1961); Munsell system.</p>
</TopicPage>
